WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.690
“Vegan burgers” are a sensible thing
to find on a supermarket shelf.

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.959
But “vegan tomatoes” wouldn’t be.

00:00:05.959 --> 00:00:08.360
In fact, those tomatoes
would sound a bit suspicious:

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:11.880
it’s the same as the old joke
about asbestos-free cereal.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.930
Those labels are ignoring one
of Grice’s Maxims,

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:18.880
a set of… guidelines that explain how we
communicate with each other.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.740
Paul Grice was a philosopher of language,

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:24.050
and in '75 he published a paper called
"Logic and Conversation".

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:29.499
He was dealing with the gap between natural
language and what’s called “logical formalism”.

00:00:29.499 --> 00:00:34.260
Basically, we communicate far more than just
the literal meaning of our words.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.840
Grice wrote that for that to be possible without
having some hyper-logical unambiguous language,

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.320
we must be operating under some shared assumptions,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.100
what he called the Cooperative Principle.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.320
We assume that we are cooperating
with each other when we are talking.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:50.940
Which is obvious, right?

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.420
But there's a deeper point there:

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.120
we all try to fit what other people say

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.810
into the context of what’s happening or
what’s already been said.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:00:59.810
That's part of why,

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:03.320
when we play a computer game and
a character says exactly the same thing

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.150
no matter if we’ve just saved their village
or burned down their house,

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.510
we can tell that they're preprogrammed.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.580
We can't find a way to fit their words to
the situation.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.909
So here are Grice’s Maxims, and if you’re
not in a video game,

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:19.900
these are the rules that we assume our
conversation partners are following.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.680
Although, despite how they’re written,

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:27.800
they are not prescriptive, you-must-do-this rules:
they’re just guidelines.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:33.560
So first: the Maxim of Quantity: give as much
information as required, and no more.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.510
Which is what we're talking about
with vegan tomatoes.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:38.340
We already know they’re vegan from the word
“tomato”,

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.340
giving too much information is strange.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.409
Next, the Maxim of Quality: tell the truth.

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:48.520
Next, there’s the Maxim of Relation: be relevant.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.980
The person you’re talking to will assume
that what you’re saying

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.140
is related to what they just said
in some way,

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.900
and they’ll try to find
what that connection is.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.090
And finally, the Maxim of Manner:

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.340
be clear in what you’re saying.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.420
Although Grice phrased that in a way that
I don’t think actually follows his own guidelines?

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:09.880
Actually, all of these maxims were stated
and restated at length.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.430
But then, he was a philosopher.
They tend to do that.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.040
So let’s have a good example of
using Grice’s Maxims,

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.660
and after that, a deliberately bad one.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.410
Let’s imagine someone says,
“I’m out of petrol”,

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.180
and in reply, I say,
“there’s a garage down the road”.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.920
Without context, just using the super-literal,
logical meaning of those sentences,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.010
there’s no connection there.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:32.880
Those are just two factual statements.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.319
But if you assume I’m trying to follow the
Cooperative Principle,

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:40.200
then you can automatically work out
a lot more.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:45.030
Using the Maxim of Relation,
the garage probably has petrol.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:50.100
Using the Maxim of Quantity, we know that
that's all I need to say in order to imply:

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.620
hey, you can push your car there,
you can buy fuel,

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.590
and you can solve your
whole "being stranded" problem.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:55.980
A problem which, by the way,

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.459
was also completely implied using
the Cooperative Principle.

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:02.310
We can bring the graphics in now, but until
this moment,

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:09.080
all that backstory about a stranded car was
implied just by the words “I’m out of petrol”.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.400
Now, using the Maxim of Quality, we can assume
I’m telling the truth

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.950
and this isn't a lie or a guess.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:18.489
And using the Maxim of Manner, even someone
who’s confused by my dialect

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:21.760
can work out that "garage" probably means
the British slang

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.280
for what Americans would call a “gas station”.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.110
It’s not just someone’s garage at home
where they park their car.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.570
That would be weird.

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:34.310
All that got implied from just the phrase
“there’s a garage down the road”.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:37.120
But what about when we don't follow the Maxims?

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:42.819
Grice says that in that case, we’re asking
for Conversational Implicature.

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:47.140
Which is to say, we are implying
something not said.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:51.650
There are two ways to break a maxim: you can
“violate” it or you can “flout” it.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:53.640
Violating it is just, like, lying.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.490
It’s breaking a maxim to deceive.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:57.920
But “flouting” is
breaking a maxim in a way

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.080
that you expect the other person
to pick up on.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.250
One of Grice’s examples is a philosophy
professor who has to write

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:08.200
a grad school letter of recommendation for
a student that they have no faith in.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.700
And, yes, I absolutely believe

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.650
that this is just Grice quietly grumbling
about one of his students.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:17.370
Anyway, the professor writes the letter using
the Cooperative Principle.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:20.620
"The student’s command of English is excellent,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.480
"and his attendance at tutorials
has been  regular.”

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.040
That is, technically, a letter of recommendation,

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.310
but it doesn’t follow the Maxim of Quantity.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:31.979
It is far too little information.

00:04:31.979 --> 00:04:35.070
It doesn’t say the student’s
actually good at philosophy.

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.610
It’s flouting the maxim,
and when someone does that,

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.280
they are hoping that the person reading or
listening will understand

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.880
what they’re really trying to communicate.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:47.800
And that’s why you see product packaging
that says “vegan tomato”

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.930
or “asbestos-free cereal”,

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:52.970
or, more realistically,
“low in fat” or “low in sugar”.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:57.230
They’re flouting Grice’s maxims to imply
that the other brands aren’t,

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:00.120
or to imply that those claims
are good things to be.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.880
And that might not be true.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.940
But it’s not technically lying.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:07.060
It’s just not playing fair
by the Cooperative Principle.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.060
Either that, or there really is
some sort of evil,

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.840
animal-byproduct tomato out there.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.470
Thanks to my co-authors
Molly Ruhl and Gretchen McCulloch.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:18.360
Gretchen’s podcast Lingthusiasm is linked
in the description, it is well worth a listen.

